==English==

===Etymology===
From English dialectal {{term|wanky|lang=en}}, alteration of {{etyl|enm}} {{term|wankel||unstable, shaky|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|wancol||unstable|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|wankulaz|swaying, shaky, unstable|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Germanic|wankōnan|to sway, be unsteady|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|wa(n)k-||wek-||wag-||weg-|to swing, be unsteady, slant, be crooked|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{term|wankle||wonky|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|wankel||shaky|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Wankelmut||fickleness, inconstancy, vacillation|lang=de}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|vanke||to wander|lang=da}}. See also {{l|en|wankle}}.

